/* book: the chapter, paragraph by paragraph (first sentences):
   - At 35 000 ft the air outside is at about 23.8 kPa, against 101.3 kPa at sea level.
   - Trapped gas is the second problem.
   - So the cabin is held at a cabin altitude: the altitude in the standard atmosphere whose pressure equals the cabin pressure.
   - Δp is the differential pressure in Pa; F is the outward force in N on a panel of area A in m².
   - At cruise the cabin sits near 8000 ft and Δp is large, so the hull carries a heavy steady load.
   - The fuselage is a pressure vessel with doors and many seals, and it is never airtight.
   - The cabin pressure controller reads cabin pressure from its own sensors, takes static pressure from the air data system, and drives the valve to follow a schedule.
   - Three protections sit outside the controller.
   - Bleed air reaches the pack at roughly 200 °C and 2 to 3 bar.
   - Why does expansion cool?
*/
